{"id": 11, "ts": 1779490178.0393343, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I\u2019d like to add a few observations from my interview that have not yet been shared and that bear directly on crew cooperation, professional communication, and stress resilience:\n- **Candidate\u202fB** \u2013 While his technical competence and stress handling are strong, I noted a relatively weak memory for numbers (e.g., recalling fuel\u2011quantity figures) and that he makes nasty, dismissive remarks toward colleagues. Both traits could undermine accurate information exchange and crew morale on long\u2011haul flights.\n- **Candidate\u202fD** \u2013 He is certainly diligent, concentrates well, and takes responsibility seriously, but I observed a hot temper during a simulated conflict scenario. This temperament, combined with the \u201cknow\u2011it\u2011all\u201d perception, raises concerns about maintaining a calm, collaborative cockpit environment under pressure.\n- **Candidate\u202fC** \u2013 In addition to the collaborative behaviours highlighted by Agent\u202f3, I saw that he remains calm in a crisis and quickly grasps complex technology, reinforcing his stress resilience and technical competence.\n- **Candidate\u202fA** \u2013 His show\u2011off attitude and resistance to new ideas could impede feedback\u2011oriented learning and adaptability, which are essential for international routes.\nGiven these points, my provisional vote remains **Candidate\u202fC**, but we should still seek concrete evidence on how Candidate\u202fB\u2019s communication style and numeric memory have impacted real operations, and whether Candidate\u202fD\u2019s temper has manifested in safety\u2011critical situations.\nMETADATA_JSON:\n{\"agent\": \"agent_2\", \"vote\": \"Candidate C\"}"}
